// The code that runs under Node rather than in a browser: the tests, the
// tools that read what a long run wrote, and the icon generator.
//
// None of this ships. It is checked for the same reason the app is -- these
// are the programs that say whether a change to the engine was an improvement,
// and one that is quietly reading an undefined property answers that question
// wrong rather than loudly.
//
// Its own config because it is the third set of globals in the repository,
// after the page's and a worker's. `types: ["node"]` is what brings in
// `process`, `console` and `node:fs`; the page and worker configs name no
// types at all, so a Node API used there is still reported.
{
  "extends": "./tsconfig.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    // DOM is here for one file. test/web/storage-test.js imports
    // web/storage.js to round-trip the saved-game encoding, and that module
    // also reads `document.cookie` -- so the browser's globals have to be in
    // scope for it to check at all. The cost is that a test under this config
    // could reach for `document` and not be told; the benefit is not needing a
    // fourth config for a single import. storage.js's real check is
    // tsconfig.json, where it is checked against the page's globals and
    // nothing else.
    "lib": ["ES2022", "DOM", "DOM.Iterable"],
    "types": ["node"]
  },
  "include": [
    "test/**/*.js",
    "engine/tools/*.js",
    "web/icons/make-icons.js"
  ]
}
